eady melded by opponent), discarding B7 or B8 risks helping someone complete a pair or pung.\n- However, **B7** and **B8** each appear only once in your hand, while **B9** appears twice\u2014keeping the B9 pair offers flexibility (e.g., as an insurance pair), whereas single B7/B8 are dead-end tiles.\n- Also, **W5** is the only non-paired mid-tile in your hand. With W4(2), W7(2), W8, W9, you can form chows like [W4,W5,W6] (but W6 scarce) or [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 you already have W7(2), W8, W9 \u2192 very close to forming [W7,W8,W9] as a chow or even a pung+pair setup.\n\nThus, best move is to eliminate non-Wan tiles first.\n\nBetween **B7**, **B8**, and **B9**, **B7** is the weakest:\n- B7 is low-count and already partially used ([B7,B8,B9] melded).\n- Only 3 B7s remain unseen; one is in your hand, one likely in opponent\u2019s meld, so few left.\n- No sign of a bamboo-based flush.\n\nSo safest and most strategic discard is **B7**, removing a stray bamboo tile not contributing to any major pattern in your hand.\n\nReason:  \nTo pursue **Full Flush (24 points)** in Characters (Wan), we must discard non-suit tiles. Among the valid options, **B7** is a single occurrence bamboo tile that does not strengthen any viable secondary pattern and poses discard risk due to existing melds. Keeping paired or higher-frequency tiles like B9(2) offers more future flexibility. Discarding B7 aligns with suit purity and reduces vulnerability.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
