ry shows the **next player** did \"Chi B7\", and the **previous player** has a meld [B7, B8, B9], so B7, B8, B9 are all public. The current discard must be B8 (by someone), and the system says \"Chi B8\" is valid \u2014 implying I can form [B7, B8, B9].\n\nBut again, to Chi B8, I must have B7 and B9 in hand. I have B9\u00d72 and B8\u00d71 \u2014 but not B7. So I cannot form that chow.\n\nUnless... the notation \"Chi B8\" means I can form a chow where B8 is the middle tile \u2014 i.e., [B7, B8, B9]? No \u2014 per the definition: \"Chi X\" means the completed chow is [X-1, X, X+1]. So \"Chi B8\" means forming [B7, B8, B9]. And I need two of those tiles in hand.\n\nI have B8 and B9 \u2014 so I could form [B7, B8, B9] if I have B8 and B9, and claim B7 \u2014 but the action would be \"Chi B7\", not \"Chi B8\".\n\nAh! This is critical: the **notation 'Chi B8' refers to the sequence [B7, B8, B9]**, regardless of which tile is claimed. But the **claimed tile must be the one just discarded**.\n\nSo if the **discard is B8**, then to form [B7, B8, B9], I must have **B7 and B9** in hand.\n\nIf the **discard is B7**, I need B8 and B9.\n\nIf the **discard is B9**, I need B7 and B8.\n\nNow, the valid action is **Chi B8**, and it's available \u2014 so the **just-discarded tile is B8**, and I must have **B7 and B9** to claim it.\n\nBut I **do not have B7** \u2014 my hand has no B7. I have B8 and B9\u00d72.\n\nTherefore, **Chi B8 should not be a valid action** \u2014 unless there's a mistake.\n\nBut the system says it is valid. Let\u2019s recheck hand: [W4:2, W5:1, W7:2, W8:1, W9:1, B8:1, B9:2] \u2014 yes, B7 is not present.\n\nUnless... am I misunderstanding the \"Chi\" notation?\n\nPer instructions: \"**Chi X**\" means the player can form the Chow **[X-1, X, X+1]**. It does **not** mean they are claiming tile X.\n\nSo \"Chi B8\" = can form [B7, B8, B9], by claiming whichever of B7, B8, or B9 was just discarded.\n\nAnd since the action is available, it means that **the last player d